<ContentPage title="Infrastructure" subtitle="The physical foundation for semi-autonomous living.">
  <p>
    Infrastructure is what separates a vision from a viable community. BARK's systems are designed for
    semi-autonomy — capable of operating independently when needed, while normally integrated with
    regional grids and supply chains.
  </p>

  <h2>Energy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Solar primary</strong> — Yungas receive strong solar radiation year-round; PV arrays with battery storage</li>
    <li><strong>Micro-hydro</strong> — mountain streams provide consistent small-scale hydroelectric potential</li>
    <li><strong>Grid connection</strong> — maintained as backup and for selling excess generation</li>
    <li><strong>Energy management</strong> — smart load balancing, priority systems, graceful degradation</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Water</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Spring capture</strong> — the Yungas are water-rich; gravity-fed systems from mountain sources</li>
    <li><strong>Purification</strong> — multi-stage filtration, UV treatment, quality monitoring</li>
    <li><strong>Rainwater harvest</strong> — supplementary collection and storage</li>
    <li><strong>Greywater recycling</strong> — treated and reused for irrigation</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Food</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Permaculture gardens</strong> — diverse food forests leveraging Yungas' extraordinary biodiversity</li>
    <li><strong>Greenhouses</strong> — climate-controlled growing for year-round production</li>
    <li><strong>Aquaponics</strong> — integrated fish and plant cultivation</li>
    <li><strong>Seed bank</strong> — preserving local and heirloom varieties</li>
    <li><strong>Local procurement</strong> — integration with Coroico agricultural markets for what we don't grow</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Connectivity</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Satellite internet</strong> — primary connection (Starlink or equivalent)</li>
    <li><strong>Local mesh network</strong> — internal communications independent of internet access</li>
    <li><strong>Radio</strong> — HF/VHF for emergency and regional communication</li>
    <li><strong>Local servers</strong> — critical knowledge and services hosted on-site</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Design Principles</h2>
  <p>
    Every system follows the same philosophy: <strong>maintainable by residents</strong> (no vendor lock-in),
    <strong>documented openly</strong> (anyone can replicate), <strong>gracefully degrading</strong>
    (partial failure doesn't mean total failure), and <strong>locally integrated</strong>
    (our infrastructure benefits the surrounding community too).
  </p>

  <div class="cta-box">
    <p class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-2">Engineers, off-grid specialists, permaculturists</p>
    <p>We need people who know how to build systems that last. <a href="/en/participate">Join us</a>.</p>
  </div>
</ContentPage>
